ymied by access to to  online learning resources so  let me say the most important point is  getting  devices and getting service in the hands  of every child who needs it there was a  herculean effort by the doe i give them  a lot of credit 300  000 devices uh distributed in very short  order while setting up remote learning  for the first time  at a vast level look that's a everyone  who has a complaint has a right to  complain everything we have to do better  we have to do better but let's stop and  appreciate our educators  and the doe and all the companies got  involved they were asked to create  remote learning for 1.1 million kids in  a matter of weeks from scratch it'd  never been  anticipated to have to do it on this  level this quickly in the history of new  york city but they did it  imperfect yes but they did it 300 000  uh devices distributed in a matter of  weeks  one of the most actually direct ways of  addressing the digital divide we've also  seen in new york city's history but any  kid who doesn't have a device will get  one for free any device that's not  working will be replaced anyone doesn't  have service will get service  uh the same methodology for letting the  don  doe know if you need it is there and  anyone who's not sure can simply call  3-1-1  but that's going to be an ongoing effort  i've heard a few people say oh whatever  device breaks we'll replace it  it's as simple as that next we have emma  from the new york times  hi mayor um i know i've been harping on  this issue but um i wanted to talk with  you about testing again  um you had asked me to go to a city site  if we needed a testing again and we did  we've been waiting for the results for  two weeks so it seems very critical to  the school's plan  to have testing that uh with short  turnaround times  what do you say to parents who you know  kid has  a runny nose or a fever and they want to  get tested well first of all i  appreciate the question emma it's so  important  i'm very 